{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310822,"nonce":"Ik06vCACXzVSdCOZXRjna/4uTWk=","title":"Mistral coverage needed no builder (GGUF says arch=llama) — the gap was the chat template; plus the plan dump was blind to the k-quant ...","message":"Pressure-test of the CURRENT arch structure before building NueGraph Stage 1's\nplanner around guesses (roadmap Stage 2 ordered Mistral first as \"nearly free\").\n\nA Mistral GGUF declares `general.architecture = \"llama\"`, so it already routes\nto `LlamaArch` — RMSNorm, SwiGLU, NORM-style RoPE, GQA and conditional QKV bias\nalready cover it. **`Architecture.fromString` can therefore NEVER return\n`Mistral` for a real GGUF.** Registering `r.register(\"mistral\", …)` only serves\nconverters that write the name literally.\n\nThe real defect was the prompt format: `ChatTemplate.forModel` mapped\n`Llama, Mistral -\u003e LLAMA3`, which emits `\u003c|start_header_id|\u003e` markup into a\nmodel that never saw those tokens. Fixed (c340faa1) with a MISTRAL kind\nrendering `\u003cs\u003e[INST] … [/INST] …\u003c/s\u003e`, system folded into the first\ninstruction, stopping on `\u003c/s\u003e`; detected by \"no modern chat specials but a\nsentencepiece `\u003c/s\u003e`\", checked AFTER the `\u003c|start_header_id|\u003e` / `\u003c|im_start|\u003e`\nprobes so Llama-3 and ChatML are unaffected. **This also fixed Llama-2**, which\nwas mis-templated identically. Sliding-window attention is still unimplemented\n(only diverges past the window; v0.2+ dropped SWA).\n\nMistral is the first model where the AMX platform gate actually engaged\n(`platform=192` = 32 layers x 6 weights). Interleaved A/B said AMX prefill was\n**+86.2% SLOWER** (16.80s vs 9.02s median, non-overlapping, 3 pairs). That\nverdict is WRONG as a statement about AMX, and the mechanism matters:\n\n`cached_f16_weight` (rayzor-tensors/src/quant.rs) dequantises each routed Q4_K\nweight to F32, narrows to f16, and **leaks the f16 copy deliberately** (\"never\nfreed: one f16 copy per weight\"). BNNS/AMX needs f16 inputs, so this is the\nprice of entry. `RZT_AMX_DEBUG=1` prints each MISS in us. Measured on Mistral:\n\n- 192 weights, **12.63 s** total one-time dequant, **+1.59 GB** RSS (6.09 vs 4.50)\n- prefill WITH AMX = 18.88 s; **18.88 - 12.63 = 6.25 s of actual GEMM**\n- AMX-off reference = **9.02 s**  =\u003e  the AMX GEMM itself is **~31% FASTER**\n\nA ONE-SHOT run builds the cache inside the very prefill being timed and then\nexits, so it pays 100% of the cost for one use. Payback is ~4-5 prefills\n(12.63 / 2.77). Consistent with the older \"~25% prefill\" note in\n[[project_untapped_compute_levers_apple]] — that was measured warm.\n\n**Design consequence: `AMX_MIN_BATCH` gates the wrong variable.** Batch size\ndoes not predict whether an f16 copy gets REUSED. The fix is to warm the AMX\nf16 cache at LOAD (where a multi-second load already happens), exactly as\n`NUE_DECODE_WARM` warms decode, instead of paying it on the first request.\nThen AMX is a straight prefill win for any long-lived process.\n\n**Benchmark rule this adds: a lever with a one-time cost cannot be judged by a\none-shot run.** Separate warm-up from steady state before claiming a\nregression. This is the 5th benchmark trap of the arc.\n\nTried it, measured it, REVERTED it (nothing committed). Two attempts:\n\n1. **Batched `forwardIds` during `warm()`** — drags the whole attention/FFN\n   stack along to populate a weight cache. **491 s.** Wrong tool.\n2. **Lean prewarm** — one batched `Q4Matmul.matmul` per quant weight, dequant +\n   GEMM only, no attention. **16 s.** The mechanism works exactly as intended.\n\nBut then the run CRAWLED and darmie observed **12.4 GB resident with the CPU\nmostly idle** — the signature of swapping, not computing. The arithmetic:\n\n    layer params 7.78 B -\u003e f16 copies 14.5 GB  +  4.07 GB quantised  =  18.6 GB\n    vs 16 GB RAM\n\n`cached_f16_weight` LEAKS one f16 copy per routed weight by design. Warming all\n224 weights therefore asks for a second, FATTER copy of the whole model. The\npre-existing decode warm then took 490 s not because warming is slow but because\nevery access was faulting through swap.\n\n**This is the structural finding of the Apple-accelerator arc: every Apple path\nwe have (AMX/BNNS f16, CoreML baked fp16 buckets) works by MATERIALISING f16 or\nf32 weights — precisely undoing the quantisation. That is affordable at 1B\n(2.5 GB) and impossible at 7B on a 16 GB box.** The unwarmed path survives only\nbecause it caches a SUBSET (+1.59 GB observed for a single 120-token prefill),\nnot the whole model.\n\nThe lever that would actually generalise is **per-tile dequant**: narrow one\nweight tile to f16 into a small reused staging buffer, GEMM it, discard — memory\nstays flat and independent of model size. Until that exists, AMX is a\nsmall-model lever only, and `AMX_MIN_BATCH` is tuning the wrong variable.\n[[feedback_gpu_not_a_cpu_lever]] — GPU/Metal was raised by darmie here, so it is\non the table; prefill (compute-bound batched GEMM) is the workload that suits it,\nand it would hit the SAME f16 materialisation wall unless fed quantised tiles.\n\nfamily is the actual coverage gap — not the architecture\nDownloaded `mistral-7b-instruct-v0.2.Q4_K_M` (4.07 GB, TheBloke). Confirmed on\nthe real file: `general.architecture = \"llama\"` (the prediction above holds) and\nit builds through `LlamaArch` with **kernels 100% pure Haxe**\n(`Q4_K_M haxe=23424, Q6_K haxe=4026, kernel_ffi=0 platform=0`). So the\narchitecture work really was free. THREE tokenizer defects, all sentencepiece:\n\n1. **`tokenizer.ggml.merges` DOES NOT EXIST** in an SPM GGUF — it carries\n   `tokens` + `scores` (unigram). `BPETokenizer` builds with `merges=[]`, so\n   input cannot merge and degenerates toward character-level tokens. **SPM\n   ENCODE (score-based) is unimplemented.**\n2. **SPM DECODE is unimplemented.** `byteLevel = (modelType == \"gpt2\")`, and\n   Mistral is `\"llama\"`, so the `▁` (U+2581) word-boundary marker and `\u003c0xNN\u003e`\n   byte-fallback tokens pass through raw. Observed output:\n   `Name:▁Threeprimarycolors\u003c0x0A\u003eAssistant:▁I▁see…`\n3. **The load path ERASES the identity evidence.** `GGUFTokenizer.build`\n   registers bos/eos under HARDCODED Llama-3 names\n   (`\u003c|begin_of_text|\u003e`/`\u003c|end_of_text|\u003e`), so Mistral's real `\u003cs\u003e`/`\u003c/s\u003e` are\n   never registered under their own names and `specialId(\"\u003c/s\u003e\")` returns -1 →\n   the new template detection could not fire → `[chat] template=llama3`. Fix =\n   also register each special under its ACTUAL vocab string.\n\nLlama-3/Qwen never exposed any of this because both are byte-level BPE.\n\nPERF (unfair — mis-tokenized input, so treat as a floor, not a parity number):\n**1.78–2.47 tok/s**, ttft ~11.9 s, load 17.8–62 s, peak RSS **4.08 GB**.\nNOT swap-bound: box has 16 GB with 11.5 GB swap already used by other apps, but\nswap did not grow during the run and RSS ≈ model size (weights stay quantised).\nScaling Llama-3.2-1B's ~83 tok/s by params (5.8x) predicts ~14 tok/s, so this is\n~6x below expectation and unexplained — worth its own investigation once the\ntokenizer is right. Notable: this is the first 7B-class model to RUN at all\n(cf. [[bugs_llama_8b_sigsegv_at_gen_start]]).\n\n**Descriptor requirement this yields for Stage 2: architecture identity is NOT\nthe GGUF arch string.** A descriptor keyed on that string cannot distinguish\nMistral/Llama-2/Llama-3, which need different prompt formats and (for v0.1)\ndifferent attention. Identity needs tokenizer-special evidence, and family\n!= builder: several families share one builder but need different templates.\n\n`[nue-plan] fusion: fused=0 split=0` read identically for \"fusion declined\" and\n\"the counters cannot see this path\". Only the ROW-WISE path incremented; the\nk-quant banded path — the one `NUE_FUSED_MATMUL` actually enables — had NO\ninstrumentation, so every pure k-quant model (the whole Llama family) reported\nzero while fusing normally. Fixed (e7150b1d): instrument the three k-quant exits\nand add `[nue-plan] sites:`, counting call sites BEFORE any gate, so\n`sites − fused − split` separates FORFEITED from DECLINED.\n\n**Rule: a zero counter can mean \"unobserved\", not \"did not happen\".** Before\ntrusting a zero, confirm the path that would have incremented it is on the\ncounted list. See [[feedback_no_silent_dispatch_fallthrough]].\n\nChasing the false zero, I ran 3 interleaved pairs of `NUE_FUSED_MATMUL` on\nLlama-3.2-1B-Q4_K_M and got **+4.7% with NON-OVERLAPPING ranges** — and was\nabout to flip the default on it. Five pairs of the same comparison gave **+1.8%\nmedian with OVERLAPPING ranges** and exposed two ~30% low outliers unique to the\nfused arm (88.4/59.9/84.2/85.9/66.4 vs a tight 83.3/83.7/82.7/82.0/80.2 in the\nSAME interleaved window — so the variance is arm-specific, not machine drift).\n\n**Three interleaved pairs is NOT enough for a default change; non-overlap at\nn=3 is not evidence.** The gate stays OFF. Note it is a no-op wherever\n`canFuseRowwise` already holds (any model with row-wise INT8/Q8_0 weights), so\nit only ever bites pure k-quant models. Output is bit-identical between arms, so\nwhat remains is purely a latency-tail question on the single-dispatch\njoined-row-space path — it survived the SpinPool floor fix\n([[bugs_simd4f_phi_intvalue_panic]] sibling work) and resembles the decode-stall\nsignature (low outliers, not a shifted median). Characterise the tail before\ndefault-on. Related: [[perf_haxe_kernel_pool_cooperation_blocker]].\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `project_arch_coverage_mistral_and_plan_dump` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}